<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raymond of Poitiers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1115–<a href="/years/1149">1149</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> was prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> from 1136 to 1149, a younger son of Duke William IX of Aquitaine who married the heiress Constance. In 1137 he had to accept the overlordship of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a>. He hosted his niece <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> and was killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> at the Battle of Inab.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers.jpg" alt="Raymond of Poitiers" width="289" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond_of_Poitiers_with_John_II_Komnenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raymond was a younger son of Duke William IX of Aquitaine and Philippa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. When his brother William X inherited their father's titles in 1126, Raymond was left landless, and by 1130 he had joined the court of King Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he was knighted. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> considered him impulsive and too fond of gambling, but respected his chivalry.</p><p>In 1130 Prince Bohemond II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> died, leaving an infant daughter, Constance. King Fulk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the Antiochene nobles opposed to her mother, Alice, chose Raymond as her husband. After Henry's death in December 1135 he travelled in disguise to avoid arrest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a>. At Antioch in 1136 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> patriarch Ralph of Domfront married him to the eight-year-old Constance, in return for liege homage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Raymond's first aim was to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from the Armenian prince Leo I, but fighting ended in early 1137. In mid-1137 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and demanded that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> honour the oath given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>. Raymond swore fealty, and John promised him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, Shaizar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> if they were conquered, whereupon Antioch would revert to the emperor. Raymond quarrelled with Ralph, had him imprisoned, and in 1140 coerced a synod to condemn him; the patriarch was replaced by a fellow Poitevin, Aimery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>. John returned in 1142 and demanded Antioch, but died in March 1143.</p><p>Raymond then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from John's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> and was driven back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. His relations with Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa">Joscelin II of Edessa</a> broke down, and Raymond refused help when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a> took Edessa on 24 December 1144. In 1145 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and paid homage to Manuel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The fall of Edessa led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> and Queen Eleanor, Raymond's niece, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-saint-symeon">Port Saint Symeon</a> on 19 March 1148. John of Salisbury reports that Raymond's constant conversation with Eleanor aroused the king's suspicions. Eleanor wished to stay and seek an annulment, but Louis took her away by force, and Raymond took no part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> that ended in defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><p>In 1149 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>, Zengi's son, defeated Raymond at Baghras and besieged Inab. On 29 June Raymond and his ally Ali ibn Wafa were surrounded; his army was annihilated and he was killed by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>. Constance, left with four young children, remarried in 1153 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Reynald of Châtillon</a>. Their son Bohemond III later succeeded her, and their daughter Maria became empress by marrying Manuel I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raymond of Poitiers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated the Crusaders at Edessa in 1146, beat <b>Raymond of Poitiers</b>, Prince of Antioch, at the battle of Inab in 1149, took the remnants of the County of Edessa west of the Euphrates in 1150, and in 1154 captured Damascus from the Turkic Burid dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second Crusade, the Zengid army of Nur al-Din Zengi destroyed the army of <b>Raymond of Poitiers</b>, Prince of Antioch, and the Assassins of Ali ibn-Wafa at Inab.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-29">29 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its army was crushed at the Field of Blood in 1119, and in 1137 <b>Raymond of Poitiers</b> had to accept Byzantine overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Inab, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=1137" aria-label="Toulouse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4585 618 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raymond of Poitiers"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=1137">What was here in 1137? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
